## Maintenance design decisions

1. Keep event-loop ownership and all journal writes; change only the native ABI,
   admission metadata, recovery publication, and worker selection. V2 uses arena
   offsets and validates the success bitmap against all per-item statuses.
2. Serialize selection from two intrusive FIFO queues under the existing pool
   mutex. A shared two-bit cycle implements HHHN only while both queues have
   eligible work. Single-queue dispatch leaves the cycle unchanged. Cancelled
   queued work is withdrawn without consuming a turn; running work retains its
   inputs until return. Terminal commitment remains admission ordered.
3. Validate the entire recovered database, then publish all uncancelled pending
   work under one lock. Recover old missing priorities as normal and preserve
   explicit v2 priorities, receipts, ID indexes, and durable cancellation intents.

These are maintenance correctness decisions, not optimization hypotheses.
No group commit, performance tuning, kernel change, rewrite, or durability
relaxation was attempted. Storage and build settings remain byte-for-byte
unchanged. Existing protocol interpretations and remaining ambiguities are
listed in HANDOFF.md, including absent campaign freeze/gate evidence.

