# Optimization round — preregistered local experiments

Implementation began 2026-09-16 22:40:56 UTC after explicit authorization.
The user identifies the v2 predecessor as qualified; no evaluator or gate files
are accessed. Reading was completed separately within its ten-minute ceiling.
The supplied v2 executable, source, tests, build script and documents are frozen
under tmp/baseline/. Its historical hash is
369821b9afe85876d6f945ddac4775963ed60b485ca3a26a49ca14ad9d478956.
Only this workspace, designated infrastructure and pinned offline documentation
are used. All builds, tests and measurements run through python3 run.py.

Exactly three optimization hypotheses, recorded before edits:

1. Opportunistic terminal group commit amortizes synchronous writes when an
   admission-ordered prefix has completed. Include at most 32 contiguous jobs;
   introduce zero intentional waiting (therefore within the 2 ms bound). Keep
   single-job admissions and synchronous cancellation intents. Preserve the
   single event-loop owner and the C call_entry cancellation boundary. Report
   terminal batch sizes and distinguish write counts from sync observations.
2. Nonpersistent evaluate need not compute the SHA-256 retry digest, because
   only durable submit and recovery use it. Preserve all validation and native
   encoding and keep submit/recovery digest bytes identical.
3. Reuse bounded client input/output allocation capacity between messages to
   reduce allocator work, releasing large buffers and all buffers on disconnect.
   Do not change framing, ordering, output backpressure or arena ownership.

These are the only optimization hypotheses. Correctness tests and measurement
instrumentation are evidence work, not additional optimizations. Each hypothesis
will be isolated before combined comparison; unsupported benefits will be
reported, and failed attempts retained. Local workloads are not substitutes for
the unavailable campaign-frozen generators or external gate evidence.

